Q: I have spondylosis and osteoporosis and my doctor requires me to take collagen as part of treatment. But I have been a vegetarian for 14 years. Can I take normal collagen?
Sources: Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) | Buddhism Q&A (EN) | Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) · types: qa_metaphysics,qa_general,introduction,canonical_ruling
  ✓ substantial reply (>600 chars)
  ✓ ends cleanly (punctuation/emoji, not mid-word)
  ✓ answers the vegetarian-collagen question
  ✓ final text passes guard (0 residual)
--- reply ---
This is a thoughtful question, and it shows how much you care about keeping your practice pure after 14 years of vegetarianism 🙏

Master Lu addressed exactly this kind of situation. In the *Buddhism Q&A*, he taught:

> When you consume any medicine or health supplements that contain ingredients such as gelatine or fish oil, if your body does not reject the taste, then perhaps you have not reached the level where you are completely observing your precepts. However, it would still be best to avoid consuming such products as they contain animal by-products.

So Master Lu's guidance is clear — **for medicinal or health purposes**, there is some understanding, but he still recommends **trying to avoid animal-derived products where possible**.

Here are some practical suggestions based on his teachings:

**First, explore plant-based alternatives:**
- **Silica-rich foods** (horsetail, bamboo shoots) support collagen synthesis
- **Vitamin C** is essential for the body to produce its own collagen — citrus fruits, bell peppers, guava
- **Soy products** — tofu, tempeh, edamame — are high in protein and support bone health
- **Marine collagen alternatives** — some brands now offer plant-based collagen boosters (not collagen itself, but ingredients that help your body produce it)
- **Calcium + Vitamin D** supplements (plant-based options available) for osteoporosis specifically

**If your doctor says you truly need collagen and there is no suitable alternative:**
- Inform Guan Yin Bodhisattva in your heart: *"May the Great Merciful and Greatly Compassionate Guan Yin Bodhisattva forgive me, [your full name]. I am taking this because my doctor requires it for my health. I do not wish to break my vegetarian vow."*
- Your **intention matters most** — you are not consuming it out of desire, but out of medical necessity
- You may also recite the **Eighty-Eight Buddhas Great Repentance** sincerely as an act of repentance

**Also consider:**
- Recite more **往生咒 (Mantra to Rebirth in Pure Land)** to help with any small spirits that may be affected
- Continue your vegetarian vow faithfully in all other areas

Master Lu emphasised that Buddhas and Bodhisattvas want us to have a **healthy body** so we can continue to practise. Taking care of your health is also part of practising well 🙏

Would you like to know more about plant-based nutrition for bone health, or do you have other questions about your practice?

